#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175692 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176044 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf8d806a-b232-5ddd-ad0b-3af70f244194" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Involving Congruent Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "bea49cf1-02d0-5aa0-9584-f2ea5a2eb30c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10584" :question/problem "![](images/bd/bdaee2dee98e41505c8a35c617a8f4fc035125ea8d504efecbe58afc9f7fd06c.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $m \\angle CBD = 5x - 5^\\circ$ and $m \\angle ABD =2x + 10^\\circ$. If $\\angle CBD \\cong \\angle ABD$, find $m \\angle ABC$." :question/worked-solution "Since $\\angle CBD \\cong \\angle ABD$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠CBD &= m∠ABD \\\\\n5x - 5^{∘} &= 2x + 10^{∘} \\\\\n5x &= 2x + 15^{∘} \\\\\n3x &= 15^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 5^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of $\\angle CBD$ is\n\n$$\nm \\angle CBD = 5x - 5^\\circ = 5 \\cdot 5^\\circ - 5^\\circ = 20^\\circ\n$$\n\nSo, by the angle addition postulate,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠ABC &= m∠CBD + m∠ABD \\\\\n&= m∠CBD + m∠CBD \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot m∠CBD \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 20^{∘} \\\\\n&= 40^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175761 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176097 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "beaec0ce-eca9-5e8c-903a-6bf152b37368" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Angles Given Measures of Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "fbd1b514-35d8-54a1-91c1-168d330811e6" :question/math-academy-id "e-2605" :question/problem "From the information given below, find all of the angles that are congruent.\n$m∠A|= 10^{∘},; m∠B|= 40^{∘},; m∠C|= 80^{∘},; m∠D|= 90^{∘} - m∠C,; m∠E|= m∠B$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the measures of $\\angle D$ and $\\angle E$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠D &= 90^{∘} - m∠C \\\\\n&= 90^{∘} - 80^{∘} \\\\\n&= 10^{∘} \\\\\nm∠E &= m∠B \\\\\n&= 40^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A &= 10^{∘} \\\\\nm∠B &= 40^{∘} \\\\\nm∠C &= 80^{∘} \\\\\nm∠D &= 10^{∘} \\\\\nm∠E &= 40^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nm \\angle A = m \\angle D\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nm \\angle B = m \\angle E\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\angle A \\cong \\angle D$ and $\\angle B \\cong \\angle E$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175762 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ca5614b-e796-5482-89c9-31410de84c33" :knowledge-point/title "Using Markings to Denote Congruent Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "bab99447-be6e-5ac0-906c-a9df9aa7f3f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-2606" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the angles shown below?\n\n1. $\\angle AOB \\cong \\angle COD$\n2. $\\angle COD \\cong \\angle BOC$\n3. $m\\angle BOC = m\\angle DOE$\n\n![](images/7d/7d0729a38dd775d0f049656b23755ace3b42d07a4040004e14942c789544c30b.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n- $\\angle AOB$ & $\\angle COD$ are each marked with a single hash mark \"$\\mid$,\" while\n- $\\angle BOC$ & $\\angle DOE$ are each marked with a double hash mark \"$\\mid \\mid$.\"\n\nTherefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}∠AOB & ≅∠COD \\\\ ∠BOC & ≅∠DOE\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nConsequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠AOB &= m∠COD \\\\\nm∠BOC &= m∠DOE\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo statements I and III are true, while statement II is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176044 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf8d806a-b232-5ddd-ad0b-3af70f244194" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Involving Congruent Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7ab3579-d44f-54f0-ab9d-afd43c98949a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174841} {:answer/id #uuid "b650e78a-86b0-5b7b-8c3f-a3e119b3651f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174842} {:answer/id #uuid "ba88261a-edd0-58a4-bdc0-6f82e25649af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "62^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174843} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ed06c6-b30a-513d-92a3-a3e22e144f57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174844} {:answer/id #uuid "5aec386b-0430-5a18-aa29-2f3bc36e26a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174845}] :answer-field/id #uuid "aceb6eeb-1f00-5f1c-98f7-83af119247db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174840}] :question/id #uuid "ee0f2311-3092-5ace-8f43-a374a26eb15f" :question/math-academy-id "q-27332" :question/problem "![](images/bd/bdaee2dee98e41505c8a35c617a8f4fc035125ea8d504efecbe58afc9f7fd06c.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $m∠CBD = 3x + 5^{∘}$ and $m∠ABD = 6x - 19^{∘}$. If $∠CBD≅∠ABD$, find $m∠ABC$."}]}
